  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What the buyer sees first</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Before any term is discussed, a buyer asks to see the shape of a single record: what one ticket, work order or matter file actually looks like. The sector decides what that sample is.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Software and SaaS</strong>A ticket thread with the customer&rsquo;s question, the replies and the resolution.</li>
        <li><strong>Professional services</strong>An engagement file: scope, correspondence and the work delivered.</li>
        <li><strong>Operations and logistics</strong>A work order, a dispatch note or an incident log with timestamps.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">That one sample is what a buyer judges volume, consistency and usefulness from. It is also the first thing that has to be cleared of personal data.</p>

      <h2 class="reveal">Signals a sector is not ready</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Some sectors take longer than others, and a few are not ready at any price yet.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>The record only exists in conversation</strong>Decisions taken verbally, or work that was never entered into a system, leave nothing to license.</li>
        <li><strong>The tool&rsquo;s terms forbid it</strong>Plenty of software contracts restrict using customer data to improve models. That clause has to be read before a buyer is approached.</li>
        <li><strong>Personal data is the product</strong>Where the value sits in details about individuals rather than in the work itself, the answer is usually no.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="reveal">Why the sector matters less than the records</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Two companies in the same sector can get different answers. The question a buyer asks is what the records actually show, and whether your company holds the right to license them &mdash; not which industry you are in.</p>
      <p class="reveal">A manufacturer&rsquo;s maintenance log and a law firm&rsquo;s matter file raise different contract questions, but both are only usable if the work is written down and still sits in the system that produced it. The sector tells us which questions to ask first. It does not decide the answer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

